{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1389993-a-perspective-on-kindred-history","id":1389993,"title":"A Perspective on Kindred History","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-05-19T09:55:24-07:00","id":1389993,"title":"A Perspective on Kindred History","content":"So basically I was struck by the inspiration and desire to write up a thing. I've been thinking about history with a VtM lense in mind, both to help me with my storytelling and worldbuilding and also just as a general thought exercise.<br /> <br /> Most of what I came up with and ended up doing was a bunch of historical reading that was basically just for my own pleasure, yet I ended up coming up with a system for describing different periods of human history from a vampiric perspective and imagining how an elder might teach them to their childe.<br /> <br /> It also got me thinking about what vampiric society may have looked like at different stages of history sense for the most part all we get a good look at canonically is the psuedo(?)-historical periods of the First &amp; Second City and then the High Middle Ages up through to the modern era.<br /> <br /> I was basically thinking about what vampiric history (admittedly primarily in the west but I'm planning on researching other areas as well) would look like from the Copper Age up to Late Antiquity.<br /> <br /> Who knows, maybe I'll make some posts about that in some time, but that's what ultimately motivated me to write up this little piece of fiction and if any of what I just brought up interests you, feel free to give it a browse. I'll admit it's not very polished, but I ultimately decided that I probably wasn't going to do more then one draft on something that was essentially just an experiment and an made to idly pass the time.<br /> <br /> Anyway, h